 s-cme-when-governments-swipe-left-australias-social-media-policy\n\nBeyond
  Blood: Transplant and Gene-Edited Cell Therapy for Neurodegenerative Dise
 ase in Children\n\n\nPRESENTER:\nNatalia Gomez-Ospina\, MD\, PhD\nAssistan
 t Professor of Pediatrics\, Medical Genetics\nStanford School of Medicine\
 n\n \n\nSESSION DESCRIPTION\nAllogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplant
 ation is standard of care for a small number of neurodegenerative inherite
 d disorders — metachromatic leukodystrophy\, Krabbe disease\, X-linked a
 drenoleukodystrophy\, and severe MPS type 1 — yet outcomes remain disapp
 ointing for most patients. Donor-derived cells repopulate the CNS slowly a
 nd incompletely\, so neurologic decline often outpaces engraftment\, while
  myeloablative conditioning and graft-versus-host disease impose morbidity
  that narrows the risk-benefit window. With newborn screening now detectin
 g many of these disorders before symptom onset\, the principal constraint 
 on outcomes is no longer early diagnosis but the limitations of available 
 therapy. This session reviews two developments that may substantially impr
 ove outcomes for these patients. The first is a conditioning strategy that
  pairs brain-penetrant chemotherapy with transient CSF1R inhibition\, whic
 h in preclinical models achieves near-complete microglial replacement with
 in weeks rather than months. The second is autologous transplantation of h
 ematopoietic stem cells gene-edited at the CCR5 safe harbor to secrete sup
 raphysiologic enzyme\, eliminating the need for a matched donor and the ri
 sk of GVHD. Data will be presented acros
